<content>
  <section><title>Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table</title>
<para>Identify the \(x\)-intercepts and vertex of the quadratic from the table:</para>
<table>
  <tgroup cols="6"><thead/>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\(x\)</entry>
        <entry>
        <para>-5</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>-3</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>-1</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>1</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\(f(x)\)</entry>
      <entry>
        <para>16</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>-2</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>0</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>16</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</tgroup></table>
<para>The \(x\)-intercepts are where the \(y\)-values are 0.</para>
<para>This occurs at \((-3,0)\) and \((0,0)\).</para>
<para>The vertex occurs where the function will create a mirror image, or be symmetrical for all points. This often occurs halfway between the two zeros (when there are two zeros).</para>
<para>For this function, the vertex is at \((-1,-2)\).</para>
<title> Try It: Determining Parts of a Graph from a Table </title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Determine the vertex from the table:</para>
    <table>
      <tgroup cols="7"><thead/>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>\(x\)</entry>
          <entry>
            <para>5</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>6</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>7</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>8</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>9</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>10</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>\(f(x)\)</entry>
          <entry>
            <para>0</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>4</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>8</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>4</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>0</para>
          </entry>
          <entry>
            <para>-2</para>
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </tgroup></table>
    <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to determine the vertex from the table:</para>
  <para>The zeros are located at \((5, 0)\) and \((9, 0)\).
    The vertex will be halfway between the two zeros. This occurs at \(x = 7\).</para>
  <para>The point in the table at \(x = 7\) is \((7, 8)\).</para>
  <para>This is the vertex.</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
</content>
